--TEST--
A `Core\Session` member called before `start` refuses with a throw the root of spec § 10's tree catches, so a program that cannot use sessions can say so -- ADR 0139 § 1
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The sibling cases pinned the class and the wording. This pins the *tree*: the
// refusal is a `Throwable`, so a request that reaches one of these without a
// session is a request that can answer for itself rather than one that dies.
uint $caught = 0;

try {
    Core\Session::get("cart");
} catch (Throwable $anyForRead) {
    $caught = $caught + 1;
}

try {
    Core\Session::set("cart", 1);
} catch (Throwable $anyForWrite) {
    $caught = $caught + 1;
}

try {
    Core\Session::remove("cart");
} catch (Throwable $anyForChange) {
    $caught = $caught + 1;
}

try {
    Core\Session::clear();
} catch (Throwable $anyForEmpty) {
    $caught = $caught + 1;
}

try {
    Core\Session::regenerate();
} catch (Throwable $anyForMove) {
    $caught = $caught + 1;
}

try {
    Core\Session::destroy();
} catch (Throwable $anyForForget) {
    $caught = $caught + 1;
}

echo "caught by the root of the tree ", $caught, " of 6\n";

// And the request continues past all six. A refusal that left the program
// unable to go on would be a fatal wearing a class name, which is the
// distinction spec § 10's tree exists to draw.
echo "and the program is still running\n";
--EXPECT--
caught by the root of the tree 6 of 6
and the program is still running
